# §1. Ask 3 — what replaces Beta\((a,1)\). The correction exponent is derivable, not fitted

**This is the ask I can actually answer, and the answer is better than an arbitrary two-parameter family.**

`03_NEAR_DIAGONAL_LIFETIME_LAW.md` §4 gives the density *exactly*, not asymptotically:

\[
\nu_{B,K}(\ell)=\frac{6^{2/3}}3\,\ell^{-1/3}
\int_{S^1}\!\!\int_B\!\!\int_K
I_{(6\ell/\kappa)^{1/3}}\,q\bigl((6\ell/\kappa)^{1/3},\cdot\bigr)\,\kappa^{-2/3}\,d\kappa\,db\,d\theta .
\]

Every \(\ell\)-dependence beyond \(\ell^{-1/3}\) enters **only** through \(I_r\) and \(q\) evaluated at

\[
r=(6\ell/\kappa)^{1/3}.
\]

So corrections are analytic in \(r\), and \(r\propto\ell^{1/3}\). Since §5.4 gives \(I_r\to I_0\) and §7 gives \(q=1-O(r^3)\), the leading correction is \(O(r)=O(\ell^{1/3})\):

\[
\boxed{\ \nu(\ell)=C\,\ell^{-1/3}\bigl(1+c_1\ell^{1/3}+O(\ell^{2/3})\bigr)\ }
\]

In fit variables \(u=\ell/L\), the two-parameter family is

\[
p(u)\ \propto\ u^{a-1}\bigl(1+c\,u^{1/3}\bigr),\qquad u\in(0,1).
\]

**The correction exponent \(1/3\) is fixed by the theory. Only \(a\) and \(c\) are fitted.** That is one free parameter more than Beta\((a,1)\), not two, and the added structure is predicted rather than chosen to fit the drift.

## §1.1 It predicts your observed drift, including its direction and its common component

A pure power law fitted over \((0,L)\) against this truth returns an effective exponent biased by roughly \(c\,L^{1/3}\) at leading order. With \(c>0\), the correction adds weight at large \(\ell\), flattens the density, and **lowers** \(\hat a\) — so \(\hat a\) falls as \(L\) grows, i.e. **rises as \(L\) shrinks.**

That is the direction both your estimators show. And because it is a property of the distribution rather than of reconstruction, **it should appear in pixel and exact alike** — which is exactly your §B finding: a common 0.064 baseline drift in pixel, with 0.169 in exact.

So your reading in §B — *both effects present* — gets a mechanism:

\[
\underbrace{0.064}_{\text{model misspecification, }\ell^{1/3}\text{ correction}}
\ +\ \underbrace{0.105}_{\text{selection, }\ell\text{-dependent dropout}}
\ =\ 0.169 .
\]

**Falsification.** Fit \((a,c)\) on both estimators across the six windows. If the \(\ell^{1/3}\) family is right, \(\hat a\) **stabilises across \(L\)** for pixel. If it still drifts, the correction exponent is wrong — try \(\ell^{2/3}\), which is what you get if the \(O(r)\) term vanishes by a symmetry of the fold I have not checked. If neither stabilises anything, the power-law core itself is wrong over this range and the whole approach needs rethinking.

**Pre-committed:** pixel \(\hat a\) stabilising to within \(\pm0.03\) across \(L\) ⇒ family accepted. Still drifting \(>0.05\) ⇒ rejected, and I will say so.

