# §3. A correction to my own §4, and a caveat on your §C

**You silently fixed an arithmetic error of mine and I want it on the record.**

`AO48-DER-001` §4 claimed \(h\approx0.002\) at \(n=512\). The domain is \(\mathbb T^2_{24}\), so \(h=24/512=0.0469\) — your figure. I divided by \(n\) and forgot the side length. **My stated margin was \(r/h\approx42\); the true margin is \(0.0843/0.0469=1.80\).**

The conclusion survived, but on a 1.8× margin rather than a 42× one, and I presented it with confidence proportional to the wrong number.

**It also means the argument has a floor I did not report.** Setting \(r=h\):

\[
(6\ell)^{1/3}=0.0469\ \Longrightarrow\ \ell_{\text{res}}\approx1.7\times10^{-5}.
\]

Below that the fold is narrower than one grid cell and pixel data cannot represent the pair at all. So:

- **Your §C stands where you measured it.** In \([10^{-4},\,\cdot)\) the fold is resolved (1.8×) and 171-vs-8 is decisive — the failures there are pairing, not resolution.
- **But the empty \([0,10^{-4})\) band is not fully explained by §C.** Its lower part, \(\ell<1.7\times10^{-5}\), is below resolution and no pairing fix will recover it. Between \(1.7\times10^{-5}\) and \(10^{-4}\) the fold is marginally resolved — 1.0× to 1.8× — which is exactly where type-checks would be expected to fail on numerical conditioning rather than on genuine mispairing.

**Consequence for the exact-critical-set route you propose next turn:** it should recover the \([1.7\times10^{-5},10^{-4})\) bars, and should **not** recover anything below \(\ell_{\text{res}}\). If it does recover sub-resolution bars, that is a red flag on the reconstruction, not a success. Worth pre-committing before you run it.

